          "trace_id": "e53ba6fd7cd76c2fd44b06c450006b67",
          "details": {
            "answer": "Ordinary least squares (OLS) finds the line \\(y = b_0 + b_1x\\) whose slope \\(b_1\\) and intercept \\(b_0\\) make the **sum of the squared residuals**—the differences between the observed \\(y\\) values and the line’s predictions—as small as possible. Squaring the residuals prevents positive and negative errors from canceling each other out and gives disproportionately larger weight to larger errors, so the OLS‑chosen line best fits the data in a least‑squares sense [1].",
            "citations": 1
          }
